WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.060
Marvel Studios and the Russo brothers have
given us several sneak peeks at Avengers:

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.720
Endgame, but comments from the directors - and
tricks they've played on the audience before

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.550
- have us wondering whether what we've seen
really tells us the truth about what to expect

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.960
from the movie.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.800
Trust is built over time, and Joe and Anthony
Russo have proven that the only thing we can

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.249
really count on from them is a well-made movie.

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:26.099
Everything else is questionable.

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:29.660
Look no further than the trailer for Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.550
One shot shows the entire team, including
the Hulk, , running at the screen in Wakanda.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.920
But when the film arrived, the shot was nowhere
to be found.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.969
In an interview with Slashfilm, Joe and Anthony
Russo admitted to changing trailers to misdirect

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:44.059
viewers, saying,

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:48.879
"At our disposal are lots of different shots
that aren't in the movie that we can manipulate

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.460
through CG to tell a story that we want to
tell specifically for the purpose of the trailer

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.920
and not for the film."

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.559
As many observers pointed out, Joe Russo also
appeared to straight-up lie about the title

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:03.929
of Avengers: Endgame when asked if it was
ever spoken in Infinity War.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:06.130
The director said no, but...

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.290
“We're in the endgame now."

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.190
When the first trailer for Endgame dropped,
reports swirled that the movie's promotional

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.390
clips would only feature footage from the
first 15 or 20 minutes.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:22.500
With a final cut that clocks in at over three
hours, that leaves a lot of the movie unseen.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.920
According to Marvel boss Kevin Feige, those
reports are, quote, "somewhat accurate."

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.360
But the vagueness of his comment leaves open
the possibility that Marvel could throw in

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.210
a quick shot or two from a later sequence.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.350
It's hard to believe that everything is coming
from the beginning of the movie.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.450
There have been plenty of clips of the team
sulking around in the aftermath of Thanos'

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.570
Decimation, but there have also been several
action sequences and a variety of costume

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:47.570
changes.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:52.600
Black Widow having both short blonde and long
red hair seems to indicate that a lot of ground

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.600
is being covered.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.980
Since Iron Man and Captain America threw down
in Captain America: Civil War, fans have been

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.480
anxiously awaiting the inevitable reunion
of Tony Stark and Steve Rogers.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.110
It seemed a safe bet that we'd get some kind
of moment between the two in Infinity War,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.530
but it didn't happen.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.920
The Endgame Special Look spot did reveal an
emotional scene between the two heroes as

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.290
they re-established their trust.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.900
“You trust me?"

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.530
"I do."

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.270
Fans everywhere rejoiced until the Russos
dropped a bomb.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:28.110
In an interview not long after, Joe Russo
announced that the shot wasn't in the movie.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.000
Anthony teased a different possibility — maybe
it's in the movie.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.420
The brothers laughed with glee as they tortured
bewildered Marvel fans with the possibility

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.940
that we still may not see Rogers and Stark
reconcile.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:43.720
It's hard to believe they'd deny the audience
a chance to see some closure for one of the

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.260
cornerstone relationships in the MCU.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.860
But if it happens, it might look a lot different
than that one particular shot.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.319
Hulk being inserted into Infinity War footage
where he didn't belong let fans know that

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:58.430
all bets were off heading into Endgame promotion.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.670
As the advance looks have trickled out of
Marvel's tightly sealed vault, the speculation

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:07.040
has swung from "who's being added in?" to
"who's being cropped out?"

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.990
The Super Bowl spot, in particular, got a
ton of attention due to a couple of shots

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.890
that seemed to deliberately leave gaps between
characters.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.380
Fans pointed out that a shot showing Cap and
the team looking up into the sky had a huge

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.200
empty area, while the first shot of the crew
striding in slo-mo through the Avengers HQ

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.750
hangar seemed to also have an unevenly spaced
gap.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:32.380
While speculation on this level would normally
be left for the most obsessive fans, the fact

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.620
that we've clearly seen two completely different
shots of Thor's axe in the Endgame trailers

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.060
has made it a topic of wider interest.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.100
Fans love seeing the Green Machine do his
thing, and yet we haven't seen him in action

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.250
since he squared off against Thanos and was
soundly beaten during the opening moments

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.750
of Infinity War.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:54.130
The fact that he's about to finish a three-movie
story arc has fans on the edge of their seats

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.700
to see how his story will play out.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.130
Many are expecting to finally see Professor
Hulk in action, while others think Endgame

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.770
may be the Jade Giant's swan song.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.840
Whatever the case, the fact that the marketers
refuse to put a clip of Hulk into the Endgame

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.290
promotional footage is maddening.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.250
Bruce Banner has shown up multiple times,
but the fact that the Russos haven't allowed

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:20.169
the slightest glimpse of green on the screen
makes one wonder what, exactly, they're hiding.

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:24.919
The Special Look trailer finally had a shot
of Thanos geared up and ready for action again.

00:04:24.919 --> 00:04:29.449
He's covered head-to-toe in armor, while apparently
"beaming down" to a location.

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:31.569
So why is he wearing armor?

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:36.710
As Infinity War plays out, Thanos slowly leaves
his armor behind, evidently unneeded now that

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.069
he has the Infinity Stones to protect him.

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:43.490
Perhaps the Infinity Gauntlet simply broke
during the snap, which is a scenario that

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.509
Joe Russo has implied as being plausible.

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:51.279
Another possibility that the scene occurs
while the Avengers are traveling through time.

00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:55.311
Time travel and the Quantum Realm are heavily
rumored to play a big part in the movie, so

00:04:55.311 --> 00:04:59.752
there's a possibility that our heroes could
be fighting Thanos in the past.

00:04:59.752 --> 00:05:01.999
"And don't get sucked into a time vortex.

00:05:01.999 --> 00:05:04.729
We won't be able to save you.”

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:09.139
It's fun to get worked up over feeling betrayed
by the Russos, but the truth is they're doing

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:11.039
it mostly out of love.

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:15.199
Speaking with Empire, Joe Russo summed up
the directors' feelings perfectly when he

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:16.199
said,

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:20.199
"The thing that's most important to us is
that we preserve the surprise of the narrative."

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:24.279
This talented pair of directors isn't just
trolling fans for the fun of it.

00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:28.370
They're aware that they're sitting on a goldmine
of information that tens of millions of fans

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:29.909
can't wait to see.

00:05:29.909 --> 00:05:35.009
They want the experience to be a good one,
not spoiled by bad timing or too much information.

00:05:35.009 --> 00:05:36.159
Joe Russo added,

00:05:36.159 --> 00:05:40.780
"When I was a kid and saw The Empire Strikes
Back at 11AM on the day it opened…

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.879
It so profoundly moved me because I didn't
know a damn thing about the story I was going

00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:45.879
to watch.

00:05:45.879 --> 00:05:48.259
We're trying to replicate that experience."

